---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { EXPORT_STEPS } from '../../lib/content/exportSteps';
---
<ArticleLayout
  title="How to Export Your Bookmarks — Bookmark Intelligence"
  description="Step-by-step instructions to export bookmarks as an HTML file from Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Brave, and Safari."
  path="/guides/export-bookmarks"
  eyebrow="Guide"
  heading="How to export your bookmarks"
  intro="Every major browser can export your bookmarks as a single HTML file. It's the same format across browsers, so exporting takes under a minute wherever you're coming from."
>
  {EXPORT_STEPS.map(({ browser, steps }) => (
    <div>
      <h2>{browser}</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5">
        {steps.map((step) => <li>{step}</li>)}
      </ol>
    </div>
  ))}

  <h2>What's actually in the file</h2>
  <p>
    The exported HTML follows the "Netscape Bookmark File" format — a decades-old, still-current standard.
    Folders become <code>&lt;H3&gt;</code> headings, links become <code>&lt;A HREF&gt;</code> tags, and Firefox adds
    a <code>TAGS</code> attribute for any tags you've applied there. Because every browser uses the same structure,
    a file exported from Chrome imports cleanly into a Firefox-aware tool, and vice versa.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once you have the file, it never needs to be uploaded anywhere — Bookmark Intelligence reads it directly
    from your device using the browser's File API.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
